WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.760
James Watt cut coal per engine to one third and Britain burned far more. My knowners watched fuel bills fall. The kingdom that bought those engines then dug and burned coal on a scale no one had planned for. This is unintended consequences, episode 141. In 1769, James Watt patented a separate condenser. The device let a steam engine do the same work on roughly one third the coal, the older newcomer design required. That sentence is the design claim.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:34.160
game. The Kingdom's later fuel ledger is the surprise. Owners who installed the new engines

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.020
saw those fuel bills drop sharply. The expectation around the patent was straightforward. Better

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.880
engines would stretch existing coal supplies further. Better engines would slow the rate

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.880
at which mines were dug deeper. Lower operating costs did something else. It changed which projects

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.460
could be attempted at all. Marginal operations became routine ones. Whole categories of

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.400
work that had been uneconomic became ordinary business.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.100
The saving was real.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.260
The total did not behave like a saving.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.740
William Stanley-Jevins, a Manchester economist, later tried to explain that pattern.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.060
The explanation starts with the problem what actually faced, not with the theory of nations.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:19.060
Britain's factories, mines, and transport systems needed cheaper power if they were to

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:19.760
keep expanding.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.820
What set out to reduce the ruinous fuel cost of the newcomer engine?

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.260
engine. That older engine often consumed more than 25 lbs of coal per horsepower hour.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.660
Picture the bill at a wet mine, hour after hour, just to keep the shaft clear of water.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.560
A machine that ate fuel that fast could pay its way only where the ore or the cloth

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.500
was rich enough to cover the coal. Everywhere else the engine was a luxury the ground would

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.360
not support. The separate condenser achieved the saving without sacrificing work output.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.360
boot. Same labor from the piston, far less coal in the firebox. At the time, every engineer

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.780
and mine manager treated lower fuel use per engine as an unmixed benefit. It let marginal

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.160
pits stay open. It let mill owners run more machinery for the same outlay. Those were

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.320
local, practical goods. A pit that would have flooded could keep men at work. A mill that

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.940
could afford one engine might afford two. Nobody in that room was trying to empty Britain

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.800
seems faster. They were trying to stop a single engine from bankrupting the works it served.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.160
Jevin simply asked what would happen once that cheaper power reached every corner of the economy.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.680
He examined the sequence of decisions that followed each cost reduction. He did not assume the

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.560
total quantity of coal would remain fixed. Watt and his partner Matthew Bolton began licensing the

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:44.440
improved engine in the 1770s. The first customers were Cornish tin mines. Then the engines moved into

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.000
the Lancashire cotton mills, drainage first, then spinning.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.640
Two industries, one machine, two reasons the fuel bill had been the obstacle.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:57.360
By 1800, more than 500 Bolton and Watt engines were at work.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:03.320
Early users reported fuel savings of 60-75% compared with the machines they replaced.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.720
Parliament extended Watt's patent until 1800.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.560
After the patent expired, other builders produced still cheaper, simpler versions.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.480
The idea did not stay inside one partnership.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.400
It became a product anyone could copy and sell.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.660
Railways and steamships had barely existed in 1800.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.280
They multiplied after 1830.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.280
Each new line and each new vessel added fresh demand for coal that had never been burned

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:28.740
before.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.380
Jevin's collected the production figures in his book and showed the scale of that demand.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.800
Total British coal output had risen from roughly 10 million tons in 1800 to more than 90 million

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:45.760
tons by the mid-1860s. 10 million tons is a kingdom already digging hard. More than

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.580
90 million tons, six decades later, is a different industrial country. The rollout did not replace

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:56.540
one fixed stock of engines with more efficient ones. It multiplied the number of engines.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.260
It extended their use into sectors that previously relied on water power, horses,

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:08.020
or human labor. A water wheel stops when the stream is low. A horse tires. A person cannot pump a

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:12.840
deep mind through the night. A cheaper steam engine did not merely stand in for those limits.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:18.260
It removed them as the boundary of the business. The efficiency gained lowered the cost of power

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:24.000
so far that entirely new uses became profitable. A mill owner who once hesitated to install

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.680
a steam engine because of coal bills, now found it paid to run machines day and night.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.700
The marginal cost of each additional hour fell below the value of the extra cloth or

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:39.280
yarn produced. That is not a story about waste. It is a story about a rational owner following

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.580
a new price. The night shift was not a moral failure. It was arithmetic that had finally

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.760
tipped. Mines that had been abandoned because drainage was too expensive, reopened once

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:55.400
watt engines made pumping cheaper. The same engine that saved coal on an existing shaft also

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:00.740
made it possible to sink new shafts. Those new shafts went into deeper seams that had previously

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:06.060
been unreachable. So the fuel saved on the old working did not sit in the ground as a reserve.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.800
It helped pay for a deeper working that had its own fire to feed.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.940
The same logic scaled to railways. Lower fuel costs per tonn mile let companies lay track

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:19.880
into districts that could never have justified horse-drawn wagons or canals.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:26.100
Each new station then generated traffic in coal, iron, and manufactured goods. That traffic

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.700
required still more power to produce and to move.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:34.360
A line built because coal was cheap to burn became a reason to mine more coal, smelt more

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.840
iron, and weave more cloth, all of which called for more engines.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.940
Jevin's noted that the very industries that adopted the efficient engines grew fastest.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.080
The saving on each individual engine was swamped by the increase in the number of engines.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.820
The causal chain is short enough to say in one breath and long enough to miss if you

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:54.080
only watch one works.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:01.040
lower cost per unit of work, wider adoption, new industries built around cheap power, higher total

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.960
tonnage mind and burned. Contemporary observers who expected coal use to fall as engines improved

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.920
were surprised by the opposite result. They had counted only the reduction inside existing

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.040
operations. They had not traced how the same cost change altered the boundary between what was

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.660
worth doing and what was not. That boundary is the quiet part of the story. An engine does not

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:28.460
decide to consume more coal. A mine owner, a mill owner, a railway promoter decides that a job

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:34.500
which failed last year's test now passes this year's test. Multiply those decisions across Cornwall,

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:40.240
across Lancashire, across every district a new line could reach, and the national tonnage stops

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.900
looking like a fuel saving program. It looks like a construction program that fuel savings made possible.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.640
Jevin set this down in 1865 in a book called The Cole Question.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.980
He was not accusing Watt of a trick.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:58.180
He was following the decisions that cheaper power invited, one after another, until the

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.140
sum no longer matched the hope attached to a single engine.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.120
The Cole Question made Jevin's briefly famous.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.660
He was invited to give evidence before parliamentary committees on coal supplies.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.380
The immediate policy response was modest.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:16.580
There was some discussion of export duties and of encouraging economy in domestic fires.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:18.720
The larger pattern continued.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.260
Later economists labeled the mechanism the Jeven's Paradox.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.060
They tested it in lighting, in aviation, and in computing.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.580
In each case, the same sequence appeared.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:34.080
An efficiency improvement reduced the resource needed for one task, lowered its price, and

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.260
expanded total use.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.280
No single reversal of the original engines occurred.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.900
The insight entered economic textbooks instead, as a standing caution.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.120
The caution is about assuming that technical savings automatically reduce resource consumption.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.560
The pattern persisted because the underlying arithmetic remained unchanged across technologies.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.960
Lower costs per unit of service leads to more units of service demanded.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:03.680
That sentence does not depend on coal or on pistons or on a patent that expired in 1800.

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.120
It depends on a price falling and on people noticing.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.500
When James Watt and Matthew Bolton measured success by fuel-burned per horsepower hour,

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.880
they rewarded designs that made each engine cheaper to run.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.560
A cheaper engine was therefore worth installing in more places.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:24.840
That rule, applied across Britain's growing industrial base, produced the measured rise

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.540
in total coal tonnage that Jevin's documented in 1865.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.160
The metric was honest, it described the engine in front of them.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.660
It did not describe the economy that would form around the engine.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:40.660
Anyone evaluating an efficiency proposal can ask which activities will become newly

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.260
worthwhile once the cost per unit falls.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.680
The second half of the question is whether those new activities will be counted in the

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.900
same ledger that records the original saving.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:55.660
If the ledger only shows coal per horsepower hour, the night shift, the reopen pit, and

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:59.440
the new railway never appear as costs. They appear as progress.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:05.380
If the ledger is national tonnage or total electricity, those same decisions are the result.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:10.220
The same question now faces data center operators who expect lower energy per

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.960
computation to reduce overall electricity demand. The arithmetic that turned a

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:19.260
fuel-saving engine into a coal-increasing system has not disappeared with the

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:24.020
fuel itself. A computation that was too expensive to run last year can become

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:27.680
the default job this year and then the reason to build the next hall of

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.360
machines. What would you watch for in that ledger if the saving per task is real

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:34.440
and the list of tasks is about to grow.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.160
That's unintended consequences for today.

